  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Pennsylvania receives $23.7B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The federal government awarded 485,410 contracts in the state, with an average award size of $48.9K. Obligations are down 12.0% year over year, and current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date). The Department of Defense is the dominant buyer, obligating $19.2B in the state. The Department of Energy follows with $2B, and the Department of Veterans Affairs obligates $857M. AMERISOURCEBERGEN DRUG CORP is the largest contractor in Pennsylvania with $6B, followed by FLUOR MARINE PROPULSION, LLC at $4.3B and THE BOEING COMPANY at $2.3B. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences is the largest industry by obligations at $5B, and Medicinal and Botanical Manufacturing accounts for $4B. Fiscal-year comparisons show obligations rose from $17.6B in FY21 to a peak of $26.9B in FY25, then moved to $23.7B in FY26. This snapshot highlights concentration in defense buying and large vendor obligations, while overall obligations are lower than the prior year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting in Pennsylvania changed year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations are down 12.0% year over year. Fiscal-year totals rose from $17.6B in FY21 to $26.9B in FY25, then declined to $23.7B in FY26. Current-year totals are partial, so the FY26 number reflects fiscal year to date obligations."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors drive federal spending in Pennsylvania?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense drives the bulk of spending with $19.2B in obligations. AMERISOURCEBERGEN DRUG CORP, FLUOR MARINE PROPULSION, LLC, and THE BOEING COMPANY are the top three vendors with $6B, $4.3B, and $2.3B respectively, and Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences is the largest industry with $5B."
      }
    ]
  },
